<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<databaseChangeLog xmlns="http://www.liquibase.org/xml/ns/dbchangelog" xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance"
                   xsi:schemaLocation="http://www.liquibase.org/xml/ns/dbchangelog http://www.liquibase.org/xml/ns/dbchangelog/dbchangelog-latest.xsd">

    <!--
        Drop attachment.exercise_id and attachment.lecture_id. An attachment reaches its owner through the attachment
        video unit alone, and this release stopped mapping both of these columns.

        exercise_id comes from the generated schema this project started from and has never had a writer: no released
        version has an endpoint that creates an attachment for an exercise, and no code path sets one. The mapping was
        there in name only. Exercise.attachments was a lazy inverse collection whose three touch points all *emptied*
        it (both import services and Exercise.disconnectRelatedEntities), two repositories declared a fetch of it that
        nothing selected, and the single reader was a ternary in GroupNotificationService that could only ever take its
        lecture branch. Nor was a row reachable: every attachment file route in FileResource is keyed by a lecture or by
        an attachment video unit and FilePathType has no case for an exercise, so no URI resolved such a file and no
        page listed one. Dropping this column removes a reference that pointed nowhere.

        lecture_id was real until this release. 20260905235721_changelog.xml gave every attachment that carried one an
        attachment video unit, so the lecture of an attachment is now the lecture of that unit and the column says
        nothing the rows do not already say. Nothing maps it and nothing reads it: the constraint was ON DELETE
        RESTRICT, which is what made deleting a lecture depend on every attachment naming it being cleared first, and
        AttachmentRepository.findAllInLecture reaches the lecture through the unit instead.

        What the column did carry until now is where a file is. The files of those attachments stayed under
        uploads/attachments/lecture/{lectureId} because a changelog cannot move a file, and since this release stores
        only a filename in jhi_link, neither the value nor this column can be asked any more which of the two
        attachment directories holds one. That question is answered by FileSystemLocation#ofAttachment, which derives
        the lecture id from the unit and, when the unit directory does not hold the file, falls back to the lecture
        directory. It is marked transitional and is retired by MigrationEntry20260907_175735, entry 1 of
        MigrationRegistry, which moves the files.

        It is safe to drop the column here and not one release later, because a deployment that carries database
        migrations is not rolling in this project: the previous version is stopped before Liquibase runs, so no node
        whose lecture import still writes lecture_id is serving while these changesets execute. The predecessor runs
        first in the very same execution and leaves nothing behind for the column to describe, so

            SELECT COUNT(*) FROM attachment WHERE lecture_id IS NOT NULL AND attachment_unit_id IS NULL

        is zero by the time this file is reached. That query is worth running by hand first on a database that was ever
        served by two versions at once, since a row it returns loses the only pointer to its file here.

        Both columns carry an explicit index created by the initial schema under the foreign key's own generated name,
        so each constraint is dropped first and its index after it. Both databases keep such an index once the
        constraint is gone, and both would drop it along with the column anyway, so the index drops are here to be
        explicit rather than because the column drop needs them, and each is guarded so that a database which never had
        the index under that name skips it instead of failing.

        This file already ran on every database that follows develop, and only its comments and the rollback of the
        last changeset are edited here. Neither is part of a changeset checksum, which Liquibase computes from the
        forward changes and any modifySql alone, so no recorded checksum changes and no validCheckSum is needed. The
        rollback has to be edited all the same: it is read from this file at the moment a rollback runs, not from the
        database, so leaving the old statement in place would leave a rollback that silently recovers nothing while
        claiming to.

        Both columns come back empty on rollback. exercise_id never held a value. lecture_id held one, and it is not
        recoverable here: the only row-level evidence of which attachments the migration touched was that their link
        still named the lecture attachment directory, and 20260906221008_changelog.xml reduced every link to a bare
        filename before this file is reached. That is stated where it matters, in the comment of that changelog: the
        rollback of 20260905235721_changelog.xml already recovers nothing for the same reason and leaves the units it
        created in place with their attachments attached. Nothing on this release reads the column, so restoring it
        empty leaves a working system, and rolling forward is the only path this release is deployed on.
    -->
    <changeSet id="20260907100622-1-drop-attachment-exercise-foreign-key" author="krusche">
        <preConditions onFail="MARK_RAN">
            <foreignKeyConstraintExists foreignKeyTableName="attachment" foreignKeyName="FK840kdpn5r9l2wyhq83gl6sx2g"/>
        </preConditions>
        <comment>Drop the foreign key from attachment.exercise_id to exercise.</comment>
        <dropForeignKeyConstraint baseTableName="attachment" constraintName="FK840kdpn5r9l2wyhq83gl6sx2g"/>
        <rollback>
            <addForeignKeyConstraint baseColumnNames="exercise_id" baseTableName="attachment" constraintName="FK840kdpn5r9l2wyhq83gl6sx2g" deferrable="false"
                                     initiallyDeferred="false" onDelete="RESTRICT" onUpdate="RESTRICT" referencedColumnNames="id" referencedTableName="exercise"
                                     validate="true"/>
        </rollback>
    </changeSet>

    <changeSet id="20260907100622-2-drop-attachment-exercise-index" author="krusche">
        <preConditions onFail="MARK_RAN">
            <indexExists tableName="attachment" indexName="FK840kdpn5r9l2wyhq83gl6sx2g"/>
        </preConditions>
        <comment>Drop the index on attachment.exercise_id that the foreign key was using.</comment>
        <dropIndex tableName="attachment" indexName="FK840kdpn5r9l2wyhq83gl6sx2g"/>
        <rollback>
            <createIndex indexName="FK840kdpn5r9l2wyhq83gl6sx2g" tableName="attachment">
                <column name="exercise_id"/>
            </createIndex>
        </rollback>
    </changeSet>

    <changeSet id="20260907100622-3-drop-attachment-lecture-foreign-key" author="krusche">
        <preConditions onFail="MARK_RAN">
            <foreignKeyConstraintExists foreignKeyTableName="attachment" foreignKeyName="FKa6apwe76xlljxtm3nimrlvppf"/>
        </preConditions>
        <comment>
            Drop the foreign key from attachment.lecture_id to lecture. It was ON DELETE RESTRICT, which is what made
            deleting a lecture depend on every attachment naming it being cleared first.
        </comment>
        <dropForeignKeyConstraint baseTableName="attachment" constraintName="FKa6apwe76xlljxtm3nimrlvppf"/>
        <rollback>
            <addForeignKeyConstraint baseColumnNames="lecture_id" baseTableName="attachment" constraintName="FKa6apwe76xlljxtm3nimrlvppf" deferrable="false"
                                     initiallyDeferred="false" onDelete="RESTRICT" onUpdate="RESTRICT" referencedColumnNames="id" referencedTableName="lecture"
                                     validate="true"/>
        </rollback>
    </changeSet>

    <changeSet id="20260907100622-4-drop-attachment-lecture-index" author="krusche">
        <preConditions onFail="MARK_RAN">
            <indexExists tableName="attachment" indexName="FKa6apwe76xlljxtm3nimrlvppf"/>
        </preConditions>
        <comment>Drop the index on attachment.lecture_id that the foreign key was using.</comment>
        <dropIndex tableName="attachment" indexName="FKa6apwe76xlljxtm3nimrlvppf"/>
        <rollback>
            <createIndex indexName="FKa6apwe76xlljxtm3nimrlvppf" tableName="attachment">
                <column name="lecture_id"/>
            </createIndex>
        </rollback>
    </changeSet>

    <changeSet id="20260907100622-5-drop-vacated-attachment-columns" author="krusche">
        <preConditions onFail="MARK_RAN">
            <or>
                <columnExists tableName="attachment" columnName="exercise_id"/>
                <columnExists tableName="attachment" columnName="lecture_id"/>
            </or>
        </preConditions>
        <comment>
            Drop both columns now that nothing maps them and no constraint or index names them. Dropping a column
            commits on its own on MySQL, so the precondition is what makes this changeset safe to run again after a
            start that was killed between the statements and the databasechangelog row.
        </comment>
        <dropColumn tableName="attachment">
            <column name="exercise_id"/>
            <column name="lecture_id"/>
        </dropColumn>
        <rollback>
            <!-- Both come back empty. See the note at the top of this file for why the lecture ids are not recoverable here. -->
            <addColumn tableName="attachment">
                <column name="exercise_id" type="bigint"/>
                <column name="lecture_id" type="bigint"/>
            </addColumn>
        </rollback>
    </changeSet>
</databaseChangeLog>
